<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Computer Science on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/categories/computer-science/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/categories/computer-science/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>Synthesis: Codecs as Structure</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Fri, 15 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/</guid><description>The series closes by restating the codes-as-priors thesis across all twelve instances and connecting the wire-format side to the Stepanov type-algebra side.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>synthesis</category><category>algebra</category></item><item><title>Your Blog Will Outlive Your Database (It Doesn&amp;#39;t Have To)</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-24-your-blog-will-outlive-your-database/</link><pubDate>Fri, 24 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-24-your-blog-will-outlive-your-database/</guid><description>Your reader&amp;#39;s words evaporate while your writer&amp;#39;s words endure. Markdown won the durability war by being boring. Every time we add interactivity, we drag back the database, and durability dies. There&amp;#39;s a missing primitive: writes that are as durable as the reads. Git is that primitive.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-24-your-blog-will-outlive-your-database/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>git-native-publishing</category><category>commit-as-write</category><category>local-first</category><category>static-sites</category><category>durability</category><category>jamstack</category></item><item><title>Bits Follow Types</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-codecs-functors-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-codecs-functors-stepanov/</guid><description>Codecs are not ad-hoc bit formats. They are constructions on the algebraic structure of types.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-codecs-functors-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>codecs</category><category>information-theory</category><category>category-theory</category><category>functors</category></item><item><title>When Lists Become Bits</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/</guid><description>Prefix-freeness is the property that lifts the free-monoid construction into bit space.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>codecs</category><category>information-theory</category><category>category-theory</category><category>monoids</category></item><item><title>TD Learning for Exemplar Retrieval: Why It Doesn&amp;#39;t Really Work</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-09-td-learning-for-exemplar-retrieval-why-it-doesnt-really-work/</link><pubDate>Thu, 09 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-09-td-learning-for-exemplar-retrieval-why-it-doesnt-really-work/</guid><description>I tried closing the loop on retrieval-augmented generation with TD learning. A trivial baseline matched the full method. Here&amp;#39;s the lesson.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-09-td-learning-for-exemplar-retrieval-why-it-doesnt-really-work/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>rag</category><category>llm</category><category>negative-result</category></item><item><title>Terminal 101: Learn Linux in Your Browser</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-18-terminal-101/</link><pubDate>Wed, 18 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-18-terminal-101/</guid><description>Type real commands into a virtual filesystem. Five lessons from &amp;#39;where am I?&amp;#39; to piping tools together.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-18-terminal-101/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>linux</category><category>terminal</category><category>interactive</category><category>tutorial</category></item><item><title>Fuzzy Inference: Teaching Machines to Think in Shades of Grey</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-fuzzy-inference/</link><pubDate>Mon, 16 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-fuzzy-inference/</guid><description>An interactive introduction to fuzzy logic inference, from single facts to LLM-generated knowledge bases</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-fuzzy-inference/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>fuzzy-logic</category><category>inference</category><category>interactive</category><category>artificial-intelligence</category></item><item><title>Watch an LLM Think</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-mcts-reasoning/</link><pubDate>Mon, 16 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-mcts-reasoning/</guid><description>An interactive explorable explanation of Monte Carlo Tree Search for LLM reasoning. Watch reasoning paths branch, dead-end, and backtrack.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-mcts-reasoning/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>mcts</category><category>llm-reasoning</category><category>explorable</category><category>interactive</category></item><item><title>What Happens When You Let an AI Loose on 1,000 Erdős Problems</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-computational-explorations/</link><pubDate>Mon, 16 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-computational-explorations/</guid><description>I pointed Claude Code at the Erdős problem database with vague instructions to &amp;#39;find interesting things.&amp;#39; It built 92 Python modules, ran 131 subagents, and computed exact Ramsey numbers nobody had computed before. I mostly watched.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-computational-explorations/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>erdos</category><category>ramsey-theory</category><category>number-theory</category><category>sat-solving</category><category>survival-analysis</category><category>computational-mathematics</category><category>ai</category></item><item><title>Free Algebras: Why Lists and Polynomials Are Universal</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-free-algebra-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-free-algebra-stepanov/</guid><description>The free monoid on a set is the type of lists over that set. The universal property says fold is the unique homomorphism from lists to any monoid. This explains why lists, multisets, and polynomials appear everywhere.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-free-algebra-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>category-theory</category></item><item><title>Homomorphisms: The Maps Between Structures</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-homomorphism-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-homomorphism-stepanov/</guid><description>A homomorphism preserves structure. fold is the universal homomorphism from the free monoid. This is the algebraic reason that fold, evaluation, and parallelism work.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-homomorphism-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>homomorphisms</category></item><item><title>Lattices: Fixed Points and Iteration</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-lattice-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-lattice-stepanov/</guid><description>A lattice has two operations, meet and join, satisfying absorption laws. Tarski&amp;#39;s theorem gives a generic fixed-point algorithm. Lattice structure determines the iteration, just as monoid structure determines power-by-squaring.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-lattice-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>lattices</category><category>abstract-interpretation</category></item><item><title>Semirings: One Algorithm, Six Graph Problems</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-semiring-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-semiring-stepanov/</guid><description>A semiring has two monoidal operations linked by distributivity. Matrix multiplication over different semirings gives shortest paths, longest paths, widest paths, reachability, and path counting, all from the same code.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-semiring-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>semirings</category><category>graph-theory</category></item><item><title>Streaming Statistics, One Monoid at a Time</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-accumulator-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-accumulator-stepanov/</guid><description>Online accumulators are monoids. Default construction is the identity, combination via += is the binary operation, and parallel composition gives the product monoid, computing arbitrary statistics in a single pass.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-accumulator-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>statistics</category></item><item><title>Building Languages to Solve Problems</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-19-dsls-sicp/</link><pubDate>Mon, 19 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-19-dsls-sicp/</guid><description>When a problem is complex enough, the right move is to build a language for that problem. SICP&amp;#39;s most powerful idea.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-19-dsls-sicp/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>sicp</category><category>dsl</category><category>metalinguistic-abstraction</category><category>programming-languages</category><category>functional-programming</category></item><item><title>Duality: The Hidden Structure of Opposites</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-19-duality-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Mon, 19 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-19-duality-stepanov/</guid><description>Many structures come in pairs: forward/reverse AD, push/pull iteration, encode/decode. Recognizing duality lets you transfer theorems and insights between domains.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-19-duality-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>duality</category><category>category-theory</category><category>automatic-differentiation</category><category>iterators</category><category>generic-programming</category></item><item><title>Seeing Structure First</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-synthesis-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Sun, 18 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-synthesis-stepanov/</guid><description>A reflection on eleven explorations in generic programming, and how algorithms arise from algebraic structure.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-synthesis-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>algebraic-structures</category><category>stepanov</category></item><item><title>RoaringBitmap</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-roaring-bitmap-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 07 Dec 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-roaring-bitmap-wire-formats/</guid><description>A hybrid compressed bitmap that picks the optimal sub-representation (array, bitmap, or run-length) per 64K-integer chunk based on density. No single prior dominates: Roaring commits to none and adapts per chunk.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-roaring-bitmap-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>data-structures</category><category>compressed-bitmaps</category><category>roaring</category><category>polyalgorithm</category></item><item><title>Alga: Algebraic Text Processing with Fuzzy Matching</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-30-alga/</link><pubDate>Sun, 30 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-30-alga/</guid><description>A C++20 header-only library for algebraic text processing and compositional parsing with fuzzy matching.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-30-alga/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>parsing</category><category>text-processing</category><category>fuzzy-matching</category><category>Unicode</category><category>algebraic</category></item><item><title>AlgoGraph: Immutable Graph Library with Functional Transformers</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-30-algograph/</link><pubDate>Sun, 30 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-30-algograph/</guid><description>AlgoGraph is an immutable graph library for Python with pipe-based transformers, declarative selectors, and lazy views.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-30-algograph/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Computer Science</media:title></media:content><category>Python</category><category>graphs</category><category>algorithms</category><category>functional-programming</category><category>data-structures</category><category>immutability</category></item></channel></rss>